          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The gospel is the good news of God’s saving reign and salvation accomplished through Jesus Christ, centrally his death and resurrection, with the kingdom proclamation as a complementary emphasis.\",\"book_summary\":\"The gospel is the announcement of the coming divine kingdom and its law proceeding from Zion, already preached to Abraham and Israel and renewed under Jesus’ rule.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Good news of God’s saving action and reign through Jesus Christ.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Good news of God’s coming kingdom and law, proclaimed across the Abrahamic, Sinai, Zion, and Jesus eras.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book counts the promise to Abraham and the proclamation to the wilderness generation at or around Sinai as instances of the gospel itself, not merely Old Testament preparation for the Christ-centered gospel. It also makes kingdom, Zion, and divine law constitutive of the message, while treating Jesus’ proclamation as its renewal rather than its unique beginning. Conversely, the entry does not make Christ’s death and resurrection the defining content emphasized by the consensus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no explicit denial that Christ’s death and resurrection belong to the gospel, and the kingdom emphasis is a recognized consensus alternative. The material tension is that the consensus normally centers and constitutes the gospel in Jesus’ saving work, whereas the book locates the same gospel before Jesus and defines it primarily by the coming kingdom and law.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the recognized category of gospel as good news of God’s saving reign, so it does not replace the referent with an unrelated one. It materially redraws the term’s historical and theological boundaries by admitting Abrahamic and Sinai-era proclamations as the gospel itself and by shifting its defining center from the death-resurrection announcement to kingdom and law. This is therefore a refinement by partial reclassification rather than a match, divergence, or wholly novel proposal.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Galatians 3:8 directly supports calling the promise to Abraham ‘the gospel,’ Hebrews 4:2 supports gospel proclamation to an earlier Israelite generation, and Isaiah 52:7 and 2:3 connect good news, God’s reign, Zion, and law. However, the printed texts do not by themselves clearly establish that the gospel was proclaimed specifically at Mount Sinai, that Zion’s law is its defining content, or that the entire earlier proclamation was merely ‘renewed under Jesus’ rule.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
